    "narrative": "Our top priority for today\u2019s slate is **Corbin Carroll**, who leads the team with an elite 81.86 score. Despite facing Michael King\u2014a pitcher who presents a challenge regarding stolen base opportunities\u2014**Corbin Carroll**\u2019s prime spot at the top of the order and a favorable platoon advantage against a right-hander make him our engine. Closely trailing is **Brice Turang** (75.06), whose matchup against Bubba Chandler is a gold mine; the combination of a high-volume stolen base threat facing a pitcher with a slow delivery provides an ideal environment to capitalize on our league\u2019s most scarce category. Both **Gabriel Moreno** and **Isaac Paredes** benefit significantly from favorable xERA matchups, with **Isaac Paredes** specifically enjoying a 19% boost in efficiency thanks to his R/R platoon edge against Shane Bieber.\n\nWhile we are deep in the lineup, **Jose Caballero** remains our most precarious start at 57.32. He narrowly edged out our other utility options because, despite the moderate platoon penalty against Michael McGreevy, his sprint speed profile remains vital for our pursuit of steals. **Dalton Rushing** is our lowest-rated active player at 40.07; he is firmly in \"marginal\" territory, and if official lineups push him further down the order or he sits, we will look to swap him out to preserve our game caps. We are currently navigating a significant portion of our bench on the IL, specifically **Trevor Story** and **Giancarlo Stanton**, leaving us thin on depth behind secondary contributors like **Vinnie Pasquantino** or **Max Muncy**.\n\nThe primary risk today is the \"Lineup Pending\" status for almost every player, which creates volatility for our daily caps. Specifically, **Mickey Moniak** carries a volatile score of 52.91; while his park factor is excellent, he faces a negative platoon adjustment against Ian Seymour, making him a high-variance play. We are intentionally avoiding **Travis Bazzana**, **Edwin Arroyo**, **Cole Young**, and **Henry Bolte** today as they lack the high-leverage context of our starting nine, keeping them shelved to ensure we don't bleed games on players with lower floor projections. Keep a close watch on **James Wood**, who despite a tough draw against Aaron Nola, maintains an elite 0.422 xwOBA; his ability to produce regardless of the SP matchup is why he holds a secure spot in our UTIL slot.",
